===Alternative forms===
* [[picalilli]]

===Etymology===
{{unk.}}, 1845, presumably variant of {{term|pickle|lang=en}},<ref name="mw">{{R:Merriam Webster Online}}</ref> which itself is from {{etyl|enm}} {{term|pekille||lang=enm|spicy sauce served with meat or fish}} or {{term|pikel|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|dum|en}} {{term|pekel||lang=dum|brine}}.

Alternatively, may be related to {{term|paco lilla||ginger-spiced pickle|lang=en}},  in 18th century British cookbook ''{{w|The Art of Cookery}}'' (1747–1843; e.g., 1805 edition) by {{w|Hannah Glasse}} (1708–1770), also referred to as “Indian pickles”,<ref>''{{w|The Art of Cookery}},'' {{w|Hannah Glasse}}, 1998 facsimile edition of 1805 edition, [http://books.google.com/books?id=BJY58UqSEMUC&pg=PA284&lpg=PA284&dq=%22paco+lilla%22 p. 284]</ref> which suggests an {{etyl|inc}} source; compare {{term|mulligatawny|lang=en}}, of Tamil origin, which also features turmeric prominently, and {{term|curry|lang=en}}, of Tamil or Middle English origin (or blend). No relation to {{term|Piccadilly|lang=en}}.<ref name="mw" />

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɪli}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|piccalillis}}

# {{British}} A [[yellow]] [[pickle]] made from [[cauliflower]], vegetable [[marrow]], and other vegetables, pickled with [[vinegar]], [[salt]], [[sugar]], and spiced with [[mustard]], [[turmeric]], and other spices.
# {{US}} A [[pickle]], typically on a base of chopped green (unripe) tomatoes, but sometimes finely-chopped [[gherkin]]s, and possibly including other vegetables.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|American pickle}} [[chow-chow]]
* {{sense|American green finely chopped gherkin-based pickle}} [[relish]] {{qualifier|particularly if used on [[Chicago-style hot dogs]]}}

